#include \f[B]\(dqbashclient.h\(dq\f[R]

int bc_state_get_func(bc_client_t *c, const char *name,
                      char **definition);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Retrieves the definition of a shell function from the bash\-server
session associated with the client connection \f[B]c\f[R].
On success, \f[B]*definition\f[R] is set to a newly allocated string
containing the complete function body as it would be displayed by
\f[CR]declare \-f\f[R].
.PP
The function sends a \f[CR]get\f[R] request on the STATE channel
(\f[CR]CHAN_STATE\f[R], channel 2) with namespace
\f[CR]\(dqfunc\(dq\f[R] and the given function name.
The server looks up the function in its internal function table and
returns the definition text.
.PP
The returned definition string includes the function body with braces
and whitespace, matching the output format of
\f[CR]declare \-f name\f[R].
For example, for a function defined as
\f[CR]greet() { echo \(dqhello $1\(dq; }\f[R], the returned definition
would be:
.IP
.EX
{
    echo \(dqhello $1\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
The caller is responsible for freeing the returned string by calling
\f[CR]bc_free()\f[R] when it is no longer needed.
.PP
If the function does not exist on the server, the function returns
\f[CR]BC_ERR_SERVER\f[R] and \f[B]*definition\f[R] is not modified.
